jobResponsibilities:
Key responsibilities: 

•	Support the Payroll/Expense central operations, system improvements and payroll system market study;
•	Build functional competencies in system business process design and principles in compliance with policies, business and statutory requirements;
•	Develop the Ops-Tech competencies through hands-on opportunities to perform system configurations in Workday;
•	Propose/ review/ execute tenant-wide or localized system changes, with the objective to achieve harmonization with the global design;
•	Liaise with system vendors to resolve product-related issues, assess product limitations and workarounds, roadmap planning and partake in new innovation testing;
•	Support new feature releases through change impact assessment, UAT, change management and deployment;
•	Facilitate user engagement forums to gather feedback on user experience to identify focus areas for system enhancements;
•	Evolve the communication framework, build a vibrant user community, and foster a collaborative spirit between users to share system usage expertise and experiences;
•	Identify opportunities for process automation and streamlining;
•	Drive co-ownership of issues and solutioning through internal partnership with stakeholders across the whole of government.

Under the supervision of the Deputy Director (Legal), your role will be as follows:

•  Advising on the unit’s legal and regulatory affairs which may arise during the course of CRU’s operations.
•  Overseeing, managing and directing investigations with respect to serious and severe neighbour noise nuisances ;
•  Determining and recommending the appropriate course of enforcement action, which may include prosecutions;
•  Assessing the type of training necessary for community relations officers and conducting training on investigative processes, criminal procedure, evidence and available regulatory powers under the Community Disputes Resolution Act;
•  Vetting enforcement documents, such as warning letters and notices of composition;
•  Appearing in Court for prosecution matters and preparation of court documents, such as charges, statements of fact and sentencing submissions; and
•  Working closely with other CRU officers and officers from other government agencies such as SPF and HDB.

• Constructing synthetic datasets from complex mega datasets with multiple variables to maintain statistical properties for subsequent testing and workflow setup; 
• Setting up and testing agentic workflows using AWS QuickSuite for automated data cleaning and fusion processes; 
• Building knowledge bases and databases that enable policy users to conduct language prompt-based policy analysis; 
• Implementing and refining interactive chatbots using large language models for policy user applications; 
• Conducting text analysis and natural language processing to enhance policy analysis capabilities; 
• Collaborating with the data science team to develop proof-of-concept solutions and technical documentation.

The officer will work with other stakeholders in the Government to formulate and review policies. 

•	Study emerging trends and assess its impact on families; 
•	Understand Singaporeans’ attitudes and beliefs about the institution of family;
•	Develop and execute engagement plans with community stakeholders to build networks and trust, and gain a better understanding of different views towards the institution of family; 
•	Continually assess and shape the definition and perception of family in Singapore, while accounting for demographic trends and evolving social norms; 
•	Conduct robust research and analysis, and engage stakeholders to understand and strengthen the ecosystem of family support services and programmes;
•	Formulate effective policies and programmes, with other agencies to address potential gaps that support various types of families; and 
•	Review legislation to ensure it remains relevant in supporting our policies and programmes.

Reporting to the team lead, the successful candidate will be part of the team working on key projects related to the management of SSG’s enforcement and compliance frameworks. Your key responsibilities include:  


Responsibilities:
•	Assist to prepare relevant data and information for analysis and interfacing with relevant systems;
•	Assist with the coordination with internal and external stakeholders for information or data related to SSG’s enforcement and compliance frameworks, where required;
•	Provide support in formulation and/or updating of enforcement related SOPs; and
•	Other administrative duties (e.g. ensure the proper filing and storing of records and information in accordance with applicable guidelines)

The Private & Shared Mobility Division oversees the development of private transport policies, encompassing vehicles, roads, and active mobility, and is also responsible for the development of strategies to prepare the MOT Family for future mobility challenges and opportunities, particularly in the areas of sustainability, research and development and inter-modal connectivity. 

As part of the Private Mobility team, you are expected to work together with other government agencies to:
• Develop and review private mobility strategies and policies, such as on the Certificate of Entitlement;
• Develop measures to contribute to the transport sector’s move towards sustainability, including measures to scale up electric vehicle adoption;
• Study trends that may impact the future of transport and develop proposals to enable Singapore to stay ahead of these trends; and
• Engage international and local thought leaders from both the private and public sectors, as well as help spur industry development efforts.

•	Build realistic, challenging, and relevant exercise scenarios that meet strategic and operational exercise objectives for stakeholders from government agencies and Critical Information Infrastructure Owners (CIIOs);
•	Provide end-to-end cyber exercise support including scheduling, coordinating, and conducting planning meetings/workgroup meetings, identifying and escalating issues and risks, developing high-quality deliverables, and exercising facilitation;
•	Capture key observations during cyber exercises and make insightful conclusions based on exercise objectives;
•	Gather and analyse facts and recommend solutions into briefings, papers or reports for senior management;
•	Support various cyber exercises including Seminars, Workshops, Functional Exercises (using cyber ranges), Tabletop Exercises (TTX), and Operational Exercises;
•	Supervise external consultants’ work to ensure that exercise objectives and outcomes are achieved;
•	Assist in the procurement of services for cyber exercises and manage the appointed vendor;
•	Develop and validate emergency response plans with key government stakeholders;
•	Review cybersecurity readiness assessment framework to support gap analysis and improve capabilities.

jobRequirements:
•	General degree, preferably in Information Technology or related field; professional certification in cyber/information security is a plus;
•	Ability to quickly understand the various types of cyber threats and attack vectors, consequences of cyber-attacks, and security operations and controls;
•	Strong project management skills including planning, coordinating, and executing conferences, workgroup meetings, and interim progress reviews;
•	Experience in planning and conducting exercises and preparing reports is preferred; knowledge in national crisis preparedness and coordination is an advantage;
•	Analytical and observant with excellent written and oral communication skills, as well as strong interpersonal skills;
•	Effective team player who can also work independently.

If you share our passion to make a difference in the cyber security landscape, take up the challenge and apply now.

All applicants will be notified of whether they are shortlisted or not within four weeks of the closing date of this job posting. 

For any issues with the application, you may drop your resume with us at csa_recruit@csa.gov.sg.

CSA is located at Punggol Digital District.

jobResponsibilities:
As an Assistant Director (AD), you lead and supervise a team of officers in the Licensing Branch to ensure smooth operations and maintain strict adherence to ECDA regulatory frameworks and guidelines. The key responsibilities are:

•	Regulate Early Childhood Development Centres to ensure compliance with the Early Childhood Development Centres Act and Regulations;
•	Review and approve different types of licence applications from Centres;
•	Guide and support officers in crafting responses to regulatory queries, managing inspections at the Centres, and taking regulatory actions against licensees when needed
•	Guide officers in handling complex cases and managing feedback and appeals from different stakeholders, including operators, parents, centre staff, or members of the public; and
•	To lead or be involved in cross-divisional reviews within ECDA and inter-agency or Ministry initiatives, to ensure regulatory alignment and operational effectiveness.

