jobResponsibilities:
For Litigation and Enforcement related work:
-	advise on legislations within HDB’s purview;
-	advise on enforcement, litigation and prosecution cases relating to residential and commercial properties;
- 	review terms and conditions of agreements and other legal documents relating to tenancies for residential and commercial properties;
-	advise on, vet and draft tenancy agreements and other related contractual documents; and
-	attend to court applications/hearings.

The Legal Counsel will also be required to work with multi-disciplinary teams in a wide range of interesting and challenging projects, must possess good interpersonal skills and able to lead and manage a team.

Procurement 
a. As part of the IO procurement team, the team will need to lead and coordinate end-to-end procurement for IO expansion and related projects, spanning industry engagement, tender specification development, evaluation, and contract award; 
b. Manage procurement processes for IO projects in full compliance with the IM on Procurement and any other related agency-level requirements; 
c. Engage industry players, vendors, and relevant agencies (e.g. JTC, HDB, NEA, NParks) to assess industry readiness and surface best practices across targeted sectors, focusing on but not restricted to facilities management disciplines such as cleaning, landscaping, and infrastructure maintenance; and 
d. Develop and execute negotiation strategies to secure favourable terms, pricing, and service levels for the Government, while preserving sufficient commercial headroom for suppliers to invest in and grow their capabilities and transform. 

Contract Management 
a. Monitor contractor performance against agreed performance indicators, put up performance reports to management, and lead dispute resolution as required; 
b. Oversee the full contract management lifecycle, alongside of the operation team, including but not limited to contract variations, exercising of options, getting approvals for purchase orders, timely renewals or terminations, ensuring prompt payments to contractors/vendors, timely collection/return of security deposit and insurance from/to contractors/vendors, and all submissions are properly documented and processed; 
c. Conduct regular contract reviews and risk assessments to surface improvement opportunities and mitigate potential issues; 
d. Review and submit contractors' annual financial and FTE audit reports to assess and facilitate bonus entitlement payments where applicable; 
e. Coordinate with stakeholders including internal stakeholders (e.g. Senior Management) and external parties (e.g. government agencies, suppliers) to advance project objectives; and 
f. Carry out any other procurement and contract management matters relating to IO.

a. Manage the IO budget to payment functions on end-to-end basis, including but not limited to tracking budget utilisation, conducting analysis, preparing reports, budget reviews and marksmanship, virements, timely recording of assets created, and updating of contract tracking module; 

b. Coordinate Reinvestment Fund (RF) and other resourcing proposals, such as submission preparation, top-up requests, and follow-through on all RF obligations; 

c. Front Programme Evaluation (PE) of IO projects, including but not limited to developing theories of change and evaluation plans, and working with line department(s) on data collection and report writing; and 

d. Ensure and track budget cutover/top-up from other Ministries/agencies where needed. 

e. Coordinate with internal stakeholders (e.g. Finance business partners, Senior Management) and external parties (e.g. government agencies, suppliers) to advance project objectives.

